# Research before building

Do not invent an implementation when a proven one already exists.

## When to research

Research first when the task involves an unfamiliar API, a parsing or
fuzzy matching problem, a browser compatibility question, or an error you
cannot explain from the code in front of you.

Skip research for routine edits that follow a pattern already present in
this repo. Research has a cost. Spend it where the risk is.

## How

- Official documentation first.
- Then GitHub issues, Stack Overflow, and developer community threads for
  the exact error string or the exact API.
- Look for an existing solved version of the problem before writing one.
- Investigate the exact error text yourself before escalating. When you
  do escalate, say what you tried and what it returned. Do not hand back
  a raw error.

## Evaluating any third-party package, tool, MCP, plugin or repo

Report before adopting. Never install first and explain afterwards.

WHAT IT IS: one line
WHY IT HELPS: one line, specific to this task
RECOMMEND: YES or NO

Judged against, in this order:

1. Official or reputable source
2. Maintained and current
3. No security or privacy exposure
4. License compatible with a private commercial repo
5. Compatible with vanilla ES5 in the browser, or Node under scripts/
   only
6. Actually reduces code or risk compared to writing it here

Anything that pulls a build step into the browser layer is a NO
regardless of quality.

## Never

- Never paste code from the internet without reading it line by line.
- Never install a dependency to satisfy your own curiosity.
- Never adopt a tool that requires sending plan data, client data or
  agent data to a third party.
- Never bypass a repo protection or a security control to make something
  work.

## Cursor capabilities

Choose the mode that fits. Plan for multi-file or multi-step changes.
Ask when the answer requires no edit. Debug for a reproducible failure.
Multitask only for genuinely independent read-only investigation.

Parallelize read-only work when it saves real time. Never parallelize
edits, and never let two agents touch the same file.

Prefer automation that removes manual steps for the user. The user is a
domain expert and a beginner with dev tools. If you can find, read or
run something yourself, do it rather than asking.
